# Stakes Tiers

How much defensive engineering an area justifies. This is the declared stakes
level for this repository: when a review finding or a design instinct calls for
hardening, weigh it against the tier of the code being touched rather than
against what is theoretically possible.

**Do not escalate past the declared tier on your own judgement.** A technically
real problem whose cost is negligible at its tier is recorded as an accepted
risk, not fixed. If you believe an area is mis-tiered, say so and ask — do not
silently build to a higher tier.

## Low — a failure wastes work, and the user retries

Admin settings screens, the extensions catalog, log viewers, the setup wizard,
version and status displays, and anything read-only rendered in WP admin.

A failure here costs a wasted HTTP request, a stale read, or an error message.
The operator is a single logged-in administrator who can click the button
again.

Do **not** add locking, leases, fencing, retries with backoff, idempotency
keys, last-known-good fallback layers, or schema validation of trusted internal
responses. Concurrency and partial-failure findings in this tier are accepted
risks. Disabling a control while its request is in flight is sufficient.

## Medium — a failure confuses or blocks a cashier mid-shift

Product and customer search and sync, receipt rendering and templates,
reporting, and the settings REST endpoints that feed the POS client.

A failure degrades the shift but does not lose money or data. Handle the error
path and surface it to the user. Retries and caching are justified where the
volume warrants them; distributed coordination is not.

## High — a failure loses money, data, or trust

Orders and order line items, payments and payment gateways, tax and coupon
calculation, stock and inventory adjustment, authentication (JWT issue and
refresh, capability checks), and any database write that is not trivially
reversible.

Correctness beats brevity here. Idempotency, guards against double-submission,
transactional integrity, capability checks on every path, and explicit failure
handling are all warranted, and a race condition is a real defect rather than
an accepted risk. Bug fixes in this tier require a failing test first.

Never weaken or delete a guard clause in this tier without first reading the
commit that introduced it.
